Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
looking ahead to the beginning of the  school year with clark county school  district which is august 8th can't  believe it's already here john valardia  is here from the clark county education  association to talk about from the  union's point of view the beginning of  school uh what teachers are dealing with  the last couple of years have been so  challenging with covet and everything  else that's been going on let's just  talk about what the expectations are  from your end for the beginning of  another school year  well we are working with the district to  make sure that we fill these positions  that need to be filled so that  classrooms have licensed professionals  and where they don't they have at least  a competent professional substitute in  place so that we start the school year  as best as possible we're looking to  make sure these schools are safe given a  lot of issues that have emerged just  this past year around the safety issue  the co-vet precautions that the district  had in place in the past they've been  modified somewhat going into this school  year but they're still there because  there might be you know resurgence of  the transmission rate of covet  and then of course we're looking at you  know how do we  try to recover lost education for a lot  of these students that they experienced  during the cove let's we'll touch on a  couple of those things in a moment again  but let's talk about vacancies within uh  the the staffing for teachers across the  district it's been an issue it was  addressed a lot and really was a concern  and a challenge for ccsd at the  especially at the end of last year it  was talked about a lot what is the  vacancy rate right now how short  is ccsd well i think they went into  this school year with  a little over 1400 vacancies okay  and i think the district is probably  close to 91 92  filling those vacancy numbers as of this  moment that we're talking for example  today they have a new higher orientation  with educators  they will continue to hire as the school  year goes on but i think they're  proceeding to fill those vacancies will  they fill all of them when the school  year opens no that's why we'll have a  need for a substitute are you concerned  or should parents be concerned with the  amount of vacancies within the district  i think every parent should be concerned  about what their classroom  is going to look like for their student  whether or not they have a licensed  professional or substitute  i can tell you we've just adjusted the  starting salary  for teachers it's over 50 000 it's made  a big difference in the district's  effort to recruit so we've seen a spike  in hiring so  my message to parents is that you know i  think we're trying to work through the  uh challenge when i say we  you know i represent the teachers but  we're working with the school district  and and trying to accomplish  another major issue that you addressed  uh school safety uh the horrible  situation at el dorado high school last  year we saw the incident at clark high  school desert oasis there have been  issues around the district that's not  necessarily new it's a big district  things happen  just this week the school district  announced the addition of cameras on  buses as a way to sort of help with  school safety issues um  is it enough what else should the  district be doing well if anything ever  happens you'll find somebody who'll say  it's not enough right i think there's  been a big wake-up call that we have to  make sure  that these campuses these buses these  classrooms  have secure environments that are safe  for both educators and students we've  seen a rise of violent behavior in this  school district  and culminating in the heinous crime  that occurred at el dorado i think the  measures that the district is taking  right now are the right ones and it's in  the right direction i think it's not a  light switch solution though i think we  were talking 370 schools 300 000 kids  i think the district is on the path  addressing the high need areas first and  foremost we're comfortable with that is  it is it enough yet no  legislative sessions on the horizon uh  what are some of the priorities for ccea  funding um we  well it's more than as usual the last  legislative session passed a new funding  formula  and it's based on student needs and  equity and it needs to be funded on an  annual basis of 200 million dollars more  and so that's going to be a challenge in  the legislative session if if there's  not resources we can't talk about things  like improving safety things like having  the appropriate staff in these schools  you know we got a big bump with federal  dollars that's what's been paying for  some of this stuff we need ongoing  revenue and most of that comes from the  state so that's a priority for us one of  the ongoing questions as you know with  ccsd when things are of concern  people talk about we should break it up  a lot of the politicians running for  some big offices around the state are  suggesting at the very bare minimum  looking at it  your thoughts on  that long discussed  thought  yeah i mean i don't think it's a well  thought out thought okay i don't think  it's a question of like creating seven  school districts i think it's a question  of let's look at the delivery system  that you have now which is one school  district make sure it's adequately  funded adequately staffed  managed appropriately by administration  and have an effective school governance  model that makes sure that their primary  objective is kids learn and i think  that's where our focus should be  um final question here uh end of the  last school year there's i think there  was a perception in the community about  uh acrimony at ccsd on the board  with administration with teachers  um and that hasn't really gone away it's  been quiet for the last couple of months  it's been summer obviously how would you  describe  just the lay of the land from your point  of view at ccsd right now your ability  to work with dr jarrah and his team to  work with the school board members and  what you're hearing from teachers  heading into this new school year  so i would describe our relationship  with administration as issue based it's  very frank and honest and transparent in  other words we don't try to get into the  politics that might be seen on the  school board with the administration  it's about hey we have vacancies how do  we resolve it we have safety issues how  do we resolve it how do we try to  retrieve lost education as a result of  the covet experience for all these kids  as well as dealing with the challenges  our educators have whether they're  overworked and don't have the  appropriate resources or they themselves  have had some you know mental health  issues because of the stress of covet we  have an honest and i think transparent  issue based relationship with  administration  with the school board trustees i think  that's a work in progress i mean i we've  been advocates since the last  legislative session to look for a fix  where we can have a much more efficient  and competent and student-focused  governing body here we're going to  pursue that  so the the narrative maybe that's out  there of what is happening in the  district and that it's  there's just a lot of problems is it is  that a fair narrative do you think that  the public has the right grasp of what's  really happening at ccsd  i i think the public has a grasp of  problems as they see it or if they're  apparent has the experience and and i'm  not going to challenge what their their  perspective is on it i will say this we  have had in the last several years we've  reorganized the school district into a  decentralized model and learning to what  that means we've gone through two years  of covad and lost education and the  challenges with that we've introduced a  new funding formula at the state level  that tries to provide equity and need  for students that's not properly funded  enough yet and we have a national  shortage of staffing starting with  licensed professionals that's not a  school district issue it's a national  issue it's a state and school district  partnership that's going to be required  to make that you know solution happen  for us so i i think that the bigger  picture i don't think everybody sees  that  my job is to see the bigger picture to  try to find those solutions but i think  what somebody might experience a parent  may experience something happening in  school i'm not going to challenge their  perspective on it i would just add to  her what the bigger picture is fair  enough john relorita from the clark  county education association with school  approaching quickly here in clark county  john good to see you nice to be here  john

